## 2. W3-P1 missing name — PASS

  Attack: `ooda launch --exec` with no package name.

  `pm_arg_name` skips verbs (`launch`), skips `--vendor`/`--from`/`--path`/`--registry`
  plus the next token, and skips every token that starts with `-`.
  `--exec` is a flag. The name is `""`.

  `run_ooda_pm_launch` then:

    if name == "" { launch_die("missing package name"); }

  That is line 57. `run_oodac_run` is line 117. Not reached.
  Empty argv name `""` also dies here.

  `--exec --json` and `--exec --vendor ../registry` with no name
  still yield `""` and die the same way.

## 3. W3-P2 name with `..` — PASS

  Attack: `ooda launch --exec ..` (also `../x`, `a/b`, a backslash name).

  After the empty check:

    if !pm_name_ok(name) { launch_die("refuse name"); }

  `cli/pm_name.oo` returns false for empty, `.`, `..`, any `/`, any `\`,
  any `..` substring, and a drive-letter form.
  Unit proof: `pm_today_proof` prints `traversal refuse`.

  The user name is never joined into a filesystem path.
  It is compared to lock `pm_tok_name`, passed to `pm_yanked`, and
  escaped into JSON. Path join uses `root + "/.ooda_modules/cas/" + sha`.
  Refuse is before lock scan, before CAS, before the gate, before exec.

  Smoke lock added:

    want_bad(p, "./bin/ooda launch --exec ..", "refuse name");

  `scripts/pm_launch_exec_smoke.oo` is 62 lines (cap 256).
  `want_bad` already refuses rc 0, `LAUNCH_EXEC_OK`, and `ooda launch: ok`.

## 4. W3-P4 yanked — PASS

  Attack: after a good install, yank `name@version` or the bare name.
  `--exec` with `--vendor` pointed at that yank dir.

  Launch slurps `ydir + "/yank"` (`--vendor` or `../registry`).
  `pm_yanked` is exact line match on `name@version` or bare `name`.
  Gate order in `cli/pm_launch.oo`:

    1. yanked           -> "err yank"
    2. sha mismatch     -> "err sha256 mismatch"
    3. extra caps       -> "err caps"
    4. else             -> "ok"

  Yank is first. Proof string: `pm_launch_gate(..., true) == "err yank"`.

  Any gate string that is not `ok` and does not start with `ok `
  calls `launch_die` with the tail (`yank`). Both the `--exec` branch
  and the path-only branch die. Exit 2. Line 117 is not reached.

  Parent `qa/pm_hostile_spine.oo` already drives this as
  `launch --exec ingar --vendor .ooda-cache/ooda-tmp/yankv`.
  That file was not edited.

## 5. W3-P5 sha drift — PASS

  Attack: after a good add/ingest, change CAS payload bytes. `--exec`.

  Launch takes the first non-`ingest_*` CAS leaf, slurps those bytes,
  and sets `live = pm_cas_id(body)` (`crypto_sha256_internal`).
  Ingest uses the same `pm_cas_id` on the payload.
  The hashed path and the exec path are the same `cas + "/" + leaf`.

  `pm_recipe_sha_ok(want, live)` is `want == live` when `want` is not empty.
  A good lock line has a sha field. Tampered bytes change `live`.
  Gate returns `err sha256 mismatch`. `launch_die("sha256 mismatch")`.
  No child. Body is not printed.

  Proof string: `pm_launch_gate("aa", "bb", ...) == "err sha256 mismatch"`.
  Hostile spine tampers `ingar` then `launch --exec ingar` (read only).

## 6. Residual (not these four)

  W3-P6 empty lock sha. `pm_recipe_sha_ok("", live)` is true.
  `pm_is_pkg_line` only requires `@`. A hand-edited `name@ver` line
  has an empty sha and would pass the sha arm.
  After a good add the lock sha is present, so this is not W3-P5.
  `--exec` still dies later if the leaf is not a `.oo` path.
  A `.oo` sitting in `.ooda_modules/cas/` under a blank pin is PV later.

  The unused `str_slice(gate, 0, 3) == "ok "` arm would accept a future
  `ok …` string. Live `pm_launch_gate` returns exact `ok`. Not an exploit.

  CRLF yank lines would miss `pm_yanked`. Hostile and SEC cases write LF.

  Hash-then-exec TOCTOU is not this wave.

  W3-P3 not installed (`--exec nosuchpkg`) also dies at line 83
  (`not installed`) before the gate. Already in the smoke. Not scored
  as one of the four named attacks.

